ight be able to use the other as pair, and you free up a tile. But B4 and B6 are useful because they are pairs, and you might draw a third for a pung, which could give points. However, for Mixed Straight, you don't need pungs, just chows. So pairs are fine.  \n\nI think keeping both pairs is okay.  \n\nFinal choice: Play W8.  \n\nReason: To pursue a Mixed Straight (8 points), I need only one chow in W suit (already have W4-6 meld), so I should discard extra W tiles to avoid forming another W chow. W8 is not needed for the planned T1-3 and B7-9 chows. Discarding W8 maintains flexibility with T1 and B7 for the required chows in other suits, and retains pairs for the final pair. It is a relatively safe discard given the tile counts.  \n\nAnswer: Play W8"}
